From: substrate
Subject: kdrive: disable pointer acceleration (1:1 relative motion)

KdEnqueuePointerEvent enqueued every relative motion with
POINTER_ACCELERATE, so the dix "predictable" acceleration scheme
(scheme 1) ran on it.  Measured against a USB boot mouse on substrate:
slow motion passed 1:1, but any sustained / fast motion was multiplied
by ~2x (e.g. a (127,127) delta became (253,253)).

That 2x is wrong for this input source and produced two user-visible
defects under a running window manager:
  * the cursor could only land on every other pixel -> "half
    resolution" feel on the axis being moved;
  * fast motion over-shot into a screen edge and pinned there, so the
    other axis still tracked while the pinned one looked "stuck".

Our pointer deltas are already coarse: the boot-protocol mouse is polled
via GET_REPORT at ~128 Hz and qemu accumulates host motion between polls,
clamping each report to int8.  1:1 relative motion tracks the hand
correctly; the acceleration only corrupts already-coarse deltas.  Drop
POINTER_ACCELERATE so relative motion is passed through unscaled.

--- a/hw/kdrive/src/kinput.c
+++ b/hw/kdrive/src/kinput.c
@@ -1917,7 +1917,14 @@
 
     if (flags & KD_MOUSE_DELTA) {
         if (x || y || z) {
-            dixflags = POINTER_RELATIVE | POINTER_ACCELERATE;
+            /* No POINTER_ACCELERATE: the predictable accel scheme doubles
+             * every delta (2x), which makes the cursor skip pixels (looks
+             * like half resolution) and over-shoot into a screen edge where
+             * it pins (looks like one axis stuck).  Our pointer deltas are
+             * already coarse (the USB boot mouse is polled at ~128 Hz and
+             * qemu accumulates+clamps to int8 between polls), so 1:1 relative
+             * motion tracks correctly; acceleration only corrupts it. */
+            dixflags = POINTER_RELATIVE;
             _KdEnqueuePointerEvent(pi, MotionNotify, x, y, z, 0, dixflags,
                                    FALSE);
         }
